    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/ABFDAFED645B6920CFDB8559C078F8A5/jobs/344113": {
      "title": "Temporary Program Coordinator- Masters of Social Work",
      "description": "Temporary Program Coordinator- Masters of Social Work You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Temporary Program Coordinator- Masters of Social Work Forest Grove Campus - Forest Grove, OR 97116 Apply Overview Salary Range $21.63 Category Faculty and Staff Apply Description Pacific University achieves excellence and distinction by investing in exceptional people to think, care, create, and pursue justice in our world. We embrace discovery in a close and nurturing environment that leads to genuine transformation. Our community is diverse, sustainable, and dedicated to discovery and excellence in teaching, scholarship, and practice. At Pacific University, we thrive in an extraordinary environment, surrounded by the beauty of the great Pacific Northwest, with campus locations in Forest Grove and Hillsboro. We humbly acknowledge and thank the original caretakers of the lands on which we live, work, teach, and acquire and share knowledge. GENERAL POSITION DESCRIPTION The Program Coordinator provides high-level administrative and operational support for Pacific University programs. We anticipate that the first three months will be in service to the Master’s in Social Work program and a  possible three additional months to the School of Learning & Teaching. Serving as a central pillar within the Graduate and Professional Division, this role acts as the primary liaison between students, faculty, and university-wide service departments on behalf of their program directors. The Coordinator is responsible for optimizing program workflows and collaborating with Executive Assistants and Budget Managers to align divisional practices, streamline event programming, and ensure a seamless experience for a diverse community of graduate scholars. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS/MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Align divisional operations by collaborating regularly with Executive Assistants and Budget Managers to standardize program management practices across the college. Serve as a strategic liaison between the division and university-wide service departments to ensure seamless communication and resource sharing. Act as a key contributor to the Graduate and Professional Division, attending planning meetings and providing insights to harmonize processes across multiple academic units. Operations Implement and manage program budgets, ensuring strict compliance with institutional fiscal policies and applicable constraints; provide regular reporting and data-driven updates to division budget managers. Process and authorize payments by reconciling invoices. Analyzing program data and assessing needs and opportunities in admissions, policies and procedures, and budget and implementing changes within policy and scope. Assist with developing and reviewing administrative and programmatic systems and procedures to ensure effectiveness of program and compliance with internal and external requirements including accreditation. Administer (and in some cases develop and implement) policies, procedures, and processes in alignment with the institution to support program operations. Create and maintain office systems and independently initiate efforts to research and change policies and procedures to assure efficiency and integrity of program systems. Assist with accreditation and school social work licensure pathway data collection and activities. Coordinate and collaborate across the units within the college to offer additional necessary support as needed. Faculty & Student Support Direct onboarding for new faculty and students across the graduate programs, including orientation logistics and clinical compliance tracking. Manage student registration and course scheduling in consultation with Program Directors. Serve as the primary point of contact for students; program policies and events; hire and supervise work-study personnel. Manage the production and distribution of programmatic materials, application packets, and new student welcome assets. Process student-related paperwork, associated with University policies and procedures, and maintain student file folders. Communications & Event Management Coordinate messaging between Graduate & Professional Admissions, Marketing & Communications (MarCom), and external partners to ensure program visibility and consistency across digital platforms and Moodle resource pages. Serve as the central hub for communication among students, faculty advisors, student committees, and external contractors. Partner with University Events to plan and execute high-stakes programming, including clinical/practicum trainings and commencement ceremonies. Direct the student entry process (including virtual and in-person orientations) while auditing mandatory compliance records. SECONDARY FUNCTIONS MSW: PROGRAM-SPECIFIC SUPPORT Manage data and information systems for the Field Practicum Program in alignment with Director directives. Monitor student practicum requirements and maintain consistent communication regarding clinical standing. Attend Clinical Coordinator meetings as a designated proxy for the Practicum Director when necessary. Coordinate and provide administrative support for faculty search committees. This includes organizing applicant materials, coordinating meetings, scheduling screening interviews and on-campus interviews, and communicating with search committee chairs and applicants. Manage all travel arrangements for candidates. SLT: PROGRAM-SPECIFIC SUPPORT Manage and analyze student records, and alert the Director and respective Program Chair about student persistence concerns Monitor student practicum requirements and maintain consistent communication regarding clinical standing. Work with the Director and Practicum Director to create and route Personnel Action Forms for all adjunct faculty . Other duties as assigned. SUPERVISOR RESPONSIBILITY Occasional Oversight: This position may provide occasional guidance or task-specific oversight to student workers, interns, or temporary staff. Qualifications REQUIRED EDUCATION AND/OR EXPERIENCE Minimum of three years of related professional experience Demonstrated knowledge of Microsoft Office, Outlook, and/or Google Suite. Proficiency with facilitating meetings or events online (e.g., Zoom, Google Meets). REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Command of written and spoken English is required. Excellent customer service and successful written and verbal communication with a broad and diverse audience in a culturally responsive manner. Ability to maintain confidential, detailed records. Proficient in the use of common office software, spreadsheets, email, databases, and electronic platforms. Ability to prioritize and perform multiple projects, track data, meet timelines, respond to others in a timely manner, and work both independently and as a collaborative member of the University with a high standard of integrity and ethics, in support of the University’s strategic vision and the division’s annual goals. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience working in a higher education setting, preferred. Experience coordinating or supporting student programs, events, or initiatives Strong written and verbal communication skills Familiarity with FERPA, Title IX, or other relevant higher education compliance standards Experience supervising student staff or volunteers WORK ENVIRONMENT Position based on the Forest Grove Campus. May allow for hybrid, on-campus, and remote work when events are not occurring. Schedule Expectations: Standard weekday hours (Monday–Friday); Occasional evening and/or weekend responsibilities Travel Requirements: None anticipated. PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS This position is categorized as Light to Medium Work. While primarily sedentary, the role occasionally requires lifting and transporting up to 50 pounds (e.g., residency supplies, faculty book shipments, and event materials). Visual Acuity: Requires the ability to perceive, analyze, and interpret complex digital data, financial reports, and printed instructional materials. Must be capable of operating a motor vehicle (including campus vans) to transport students and guest authors during residency periods. Employees must be able to perform the essential functions of the position satisfactorily. Upon request, reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform these functions, absent undue hardship. APPLICATION PROCESS To apply, please upload your resume, cover letter and three professional references along with your Paycom application.  Persons who do not follow and complete the application process are not applicants and will not be considered for employment. Only qualified applicants will be contacted for an interview. During the selection process, we will strive to provide timely notifications. The selection process may vary from recruitment to recruitment but typically includes multiple interviews and may take several weeks depending on the position. All qualified applicants will be notified once the selection process has closed. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and continue to work without employer visa sponsorship, now or in the future. At Pacific University, we are dedicated to offering experiences that are authentic, holistic, equity-minded, and responsive. Our communal success rests on the recognition that complex problems and effective solutions are best approached by teams with a variety of diverse perspectives. and life experiences. As we continue to develop our equity practices, we actively promote access to opportunities and equitable pay for all employees. Studies have shown that women and people of color are less likely to apply for jobs where they are unsure if they can perform every function in the job description. We are most interested in finding the best candidate for the job and that candidate may come from a variety of backgrounds and lived experiences. If you are interested in applying, we encourage you to think broadly about how your background and skill set may qualify you for the role. We, who make up Pacific University, aspire to become a community of purpose where everyone is enabled to be wholly and comfortably who they are; accountable for inclusive behaviors and practices; built on trust, civility, care, openness, and honest conversations often around critical subjects. We know that these values are critical to prepare for a global world with complex problems, through an asset-based mindset so that everyone is afforded an opportunity to succeed. We believe in fostering a workplace where every employee is respected, their contributions are valued, and they are provided opportunities to succeed. ACCOMMODATIONS & INCLUSION We welcome and encourage applications from neurodivergent individuals and people with disabilities. If you need any accommodations or support at any stage of the application or interview process such as receiving interview questions in advance, alternative assessment formats, or extra time please let us know by emailing Catheryn Coupland ccoupland@pacificu.edu We are committed to providing an accessible process that allows you to demonstrate your strengths. EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYMENT Pacific University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and is committed to complying with all applicable laws, including those concerning employment and workplace policies. We are committed to fostering a non-discriminatory and inclusive work environment. We are proud to encourage applications from individuals of all backgrounds, including those from historically underrepresented groups. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATION POLICY Pacific University makes all employment decisions without regard to race, color, national or ethnic origin, ancestry, age, religion or religious creed, disability, sex, gender identity and/or expression, sexual orientation, military or veteran status, or any other characteristics protected by applicable law. Applicable laws include, but are not limited to, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination Act, and the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990. Questions or complaints may be directed to the Vice President for Academic Affairs at 2043 College Way, Forest Grove, OR 97116 or via email at provost@pacificu.edu. Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/ABFDAFED645B6920CFDB8559C078F8A5/jobs/337501": {
      "title": "MSW Program Director & Professor",
      "description": "MSW Program Director & Professor You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account MSW Program Director & Professor Forest Grove Campus - Forest Grove, OR 97116 Apply Overview Salary Range $85,000.00 - $117,000.00 Position Type Full Time Category Faculty and Staff Apply Description Pacific University achieves excellence and distinction by investing in exceptional people to think, care, create, and pursue justice in our world. We embrace discovery in a close and nurturing environment that leads to genuine transformation. Our community is diverse, sustainable, and dedicated to discovery and excellence in teaching, scholarship, and practice. At Pacific University, we thrive in an extraordinary environment, surrounded by the beauty of the great Pacific Northwest, with campus locations in Forest Grove and Hillsboro. We humbly acknowledge and thank the original caretakers of the lands on which we live, work, teach, and acquire and share knowledge. GENERAL DESCRIPTION OF POSITION: The MSW Program Director is a 12-month, in-person, tenure-track faculty position with academic and administrative duties. The Director is responsible for the overall administration, development, and evaluation of the Master of Social Work (MSW) program. This position ensures compliance with the Council on Social Work Education (CSWE) accreditation standards and supports student learning by providing leadership and oversight for all components of the MSW Program. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS/MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES: The MSW Program Director provides leadership and oversight for faculty, staff, practicum education personnel, and other program resources, as applicable. The position establishes priorities, assigns responsibilities, evaluates performance, addresses operational and personnel concerns, and ensures that program activities are carried out effectively and in accordance with institutional and CSWE standards. The Director may participate in recruitment, selection, onboarding, development, and corrective-action processes consistent with institutional policy and delegated authority. Provide vision and leadership for the MSW Program Foster a community of inclusion in all aspects of departmental functions, including instruction, service, scholarship, advising, administrative work and collaboration with students Teaching load of 9 credits per academic year Develop strong relationships with internal and external parties to promote the MSW in-person and online components and to support the mission of the Program Ensure compliance with institutional policies and accreditation standards Plan, monitor, and administer the program budget within established institutional policies and delegated authority. Lead curriculum review, assessment, program evaluation, accreditation activities, and continuous quality-improvement efforts Participates in student recruitment, admissions, orientation, advising, retention, and graduation activities Contribute to recruitment, supervision, and evaluation of faculty and staff Facilitate and support the faculty in long-range planning and program development Guide the faculty in outcomes assessment and curriculum review Recruit, supervise, and evaluate administrative personnel Implement standards for student performance and advancement Represent the Program in the University’s administrative structure Represent the Program on local, state, and national levels within the professions and the community SECONDARY FUNCTIONS: Performs other duties as assigned that are reasonably related to the position and the mission of the program and institution. JOB SCOPE: The MSW Program Director has broad responsibility for the academic, administrative, and operational leadership of the Master of Social Work (MSW) program. The position operates within applicable university policies, accreditation standards, regulatory requirements, and established institutional procedures, while exercising substantial independent judgment in interpreting and applying those requirements to program operations. The position is responsible for developing, implementing, evaluating, and recommending new policies, procedures, guidelines, and program initiatives necessary to maintain academic quality, accreditation compliance, student success, and alignment with the evolving needs of the social work profession. The Director has authority to make day-to-day decisions within established parameters and recommends significant policy or program changes through appropriate institutional governance processes. SUPERVISOR RESPONSIBILITY: Provide direct supervision and ongoing support to the Program Coordinator through regular weekly check-ins, goal setting, performance feedback and annual performance evaluations. Supervise and provide guidance to student employees, including assigning work, monitoring performance, and ensuring responsibilities are completed effectively. Qualifications EDUCATION AND/OR EXPERIENCE: PhD or DSW degree from a CSWE accredited institution. Possess or be eligible to hold an Oregon Social Work License (LCSW or LMSW). A minimum of five years of full-time higher education experience, with a minimum of three years of full-time experience as a core faculty member in a CSWE accredited program. A minimum of three years of full-time administrative social work experience. Academic experience allowing the candidate to be eligible for Associate or Full Professor status. Evidence of increasing administrative, management, and budgetary responsibilities. Outstanding leadership, interpersonal, and communication skills. Management style which emphasizes open communication, collaborative work relations, and continuous quality improvement. Knowledge of curriculum design according to CSWE accreditation standards. Service to the community and the social work profession. Familiarity with CSWE Practicum Education requirements and assessment. REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, ABILITIES & BEHAVIORS: Communicates with students, faculty, staff and the public appropriate to the age of the individual served. Builds on basic learning and carries it from one situation to another. Demonstrates critical thinking skills in problem solving and decision-making. Asks questions and seeks assistance. Takes responsibility to eliminate the source of problems. Demonstrates confidentiality regarding information pertaining to all students, faculty and staff. Maintains proper appearance and personal conduct for the employee’s particular job. Uses effective communication skills taking into consideration body language, filters, listening, paraphrasing, and questions with customers of diverse ethnic and cultural backgrounds. Demonstrates ability to work in a team environment. PHYSICAL ACTIVITIES: __Climbing _X_Balancing _X_Stooping _X_Kneeling __Crouching __Crawling _X_Reaching _X_Standing  _X_Walking _X_Pushing _X_Pulling __X Lifting _X_Talking _X_Hearing  X__Repetitive motion PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS: _X_Sedentary work _X_Light work __Medium work __Heavy work __Very Heavy work _X_Visual acuity APPLICATION PROCESS: To apply, please upload your resume, cover letter and three professional references along with your Paycom application.  Persons who do not follow and complete the application process are not applicants and will not be considered for employment. Only qualified applicants will be contacted for an interview. During the selection process, we will strive to provide timely notifications. The selection process may vary from recruitment to recruitment but typically includes multiple interviews and may take several weeks depending on the position. All qualified applicants will be notified once the selection process has closed. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and continue to work without employer visa sponsorship, now or in the future. At Pacific University, we are dedicated to offering experiences that are authentic, holistic, equity-minded, and responsive. Our communal success rests on the recognition that complex problems and effective solutions are best approached by teams with a variety of diverse perspectives. and life experiences. As we continue to develop our equity practices, we actively promote access to opportunities and equitable pay for all employees. Studies have shown that women and people of color are less likely to apply for jobs where they are unsure if they can perform every function in the job description. We are most interested in finding the best candidate for the job and that candidate may come from a variety of backgrounds and lived experiences. If you are interested in applying, we encourage you to think broadly about how your background and skill set may qualify you for the role. We, who make up Pacific University, aspire to become a community of purpose where everyone is enabled to be wholly and comfortably who they are; accountable for inclusive behaviors and practices; built on trust, civility, care, openness, and honest conversations often around critical subjects. We know that these values are critical to prepare for a global world with complex problems, through an asset-based mindset so that everyone is afforded an opportunity to succeed. We believe in fostering a workplace where every employee is respected, their contributions are valued, and they are provided opportunities to succeed. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS: We offer a comprehensive benefits package for qualifying employees. Current benefits include: Generous paid time off (for vacation, holidays—including winter break—and sick leave) Medical coverage with alternative care options (with in-network services covered at 100% after payroll deduction) Comprehensive dental and vision plans Generous retirement contribution after one year of service with no match required Free mental health appointments Tuition remission for employees and their dependent child(ren) who meet eligibility requirements On-campus benefits, including free access to our sports facility and library Discounts on campus food, bookstore purchases, clinic services. ACCOMMODATIONS & INCLUSION: We welcome and encourage applications from neurodivergent individuals and people with disabilities. If you need any accommodations or support at any stage of the application or interview process such as receiving interview questions in advance, alternative assessment formats, or extra time please let us know by emailing Catheryn Coupland ccoupland@pacificu.edu We are committed to providing an accessible process that allows you to demonstrate your strengths. EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYMENT: Pacific University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and is committed to complying with all applicable laws, including those concerning employment and workplace policies. We are committed to fostering a non-discriminatory and inclusive work environment. We are proud to encourage applications from individuals of all backgrounds, including those from historically underrepresented groups. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATION POLICY: Pacific University makes all employment decisions without regard to race, color, national or ethnic origin, ancestry, age, religion or religious creed, disability, sex, gender identity and/or expression, sexual orientation, military or veteran status, or any other characteristics protected by applicable law. Applicable laws include, but are not limited to, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination Act, and the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990. Questions or complaints may be directed to the Vice President for Academic Affairs at 2043 College Way, Forest Grove, OR 97116 or via email at provost@pacificu.edu . Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
